{"id":"h268","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-chemistry","question":"A saturated solution of the strong electrolyte Ca(OH)_2 is prepared by adding sufficient water to 5.0 × 10^-4 mole of Ca(OH)_2 to form 100 ml of solution. What is the pH of this solution?\nIs the correct answer \"12\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Ca(OH)2 is a strong electrolyte and dissociates completely into Ca2+ and 2 OH-.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The concentration of Ca(OH)2 is 5.0 × 10^-3 M.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.7,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"[OH-] is 1.0 × 10^-2 M, twice the Ca(OH)2 concentration.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"pOH is 2.00 and pH is 12.00.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.9,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Water autoionization contributes negligibly at this OH- concentration.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The amount of Ca(OH)2 is about 0.037 g in 100 mL, roughly 0.37 g/L, below its solubility of about 1.7 g/L, so the solution is actually unsaturated.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The 'saturated' wording is inconsistent with the numbers, but this does not change the answer because pH depends only on the amount dissolved.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.6,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The result is 12.00 rather than just 12, but this is only a reporting detail and 12 matches to the given precision.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The correct answer is 12.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0